      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance composed of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater molecules are formed when two hydrogen atoms bond to a single oxygen atom. This bond creates a \"V\" shape. Because oxygen is more electronegative than hydrogen, the oxygen atom pulls the shared electrons closer to itself, giving the oxygen a slight negative charge and the hydrogens a slight positive charge. This makes water a **polar molecule**, meaning it has a positive and a negative end.\n\n### 2. Physical Properties\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Because of its polarity, water is excellent at dissolving other substances. It can dissolve more substances than any other liquid, which is why it is essential for transporting nutrients in the blood and plants.\n*   **States of Matter:** Water is unique because it can exist as a solid (ice), a liquid, and a gas (water vapor) all at the same temperature (0°C or 32°F). This is due to hydrogen bonds that keep molecules loosely packed in ice and allow them to move freely in steam.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together strongly at the surface, which allows small insects to walk on water and creates droplets rather than spreading out flat.\n\n### 3. Importance to Life\nWater is the foundation of life on Earth. It is essential for:\n*   **Biological Functions:** It acts as a medium for chemical reactions in cells and helps regulate body temperature through sweating and panting.\n*   **Transport:** It moves nutrients and waste products through living organisms.\n*   **Ecosystems:** It supports all known forms of life, from microscopic bacteria to blue whales.\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with complex properties that make it the most vital substance for sustaining life on our planet."
